1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,240
A connection line alone cannot decide whether
it reaches an object.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:09,320
Represent the target occupied range with
spheres and boxes.

3
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:17,320
First, use distance to test a sphere. Next,
build a box from point minima and maxima

4
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:21,540
and compare overlap. Finally, rotate the same
box and calculate its changed bounds.

5
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:24,607
In Serious Sam2, round objects approach and
bright explosions appear.

6
00:00:24,607 --> 00:00:29,247
First find the rounded outlines.

7
00:00:29,755 --> 00:00:33,615
A center and radius offer a simple calculation

8
00:00:33,855 --> 00:00:37,283
model: the same distance in every direction.

9
00:00:37,603 --> 00:00:42,783
A rounded visual effect is not proof of a
spherical surface or damage region.

10
00:00:43,103 --> 00:00:45,903
Radius belongs to explicitly defined
calculation data.

11
00:00:46,451 --> 00:00:49,671
A target closer than the radius is inside;
equal distance is on the boundary.

12
00:00:49,671 --> 00:00:53,591
Our diagram will distinguish three cases.

13
00:00:54,499 --> 00:00:59,099
Growing screen size as an object approaches is
different from changing its actual radius.

14
00:00:59,439 --> 00:01:02,239
Perspective pixels do not establish world
size.

15
00:01:02,787 --> 00:01:05,727
A sphere is an initial approximation for a
complex rounded object.

16
00:01:06,087 --> 00:01:09,307
Specify what must be enclosed and the
acceptable approximation error.

17
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:12,267
We have changed to a new shot of the blue
enemy.

18
00:01:13,007 --> 00:01:16,367
Compare its round head and long limbs using
the red outlines.

19
00:01:16,867 --> 00:01:22,847
The teal round range is an illustrative screen
comparison,

20
00:01:23,247 --> 00:01:27,547
not the game collision sphere. Next, define a
center and radius to test a sphere.

21
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:30,947
Move the circle distance test into space.

22
00:01:31,147 --> 00:01:35,367
A spatial point uses three coordinates, x, y
and z.

23
00:01:35,727 --> 00:01:40,527
An offset of two along one axis and zero along
the others has

24
00:01:40,747 --> 00:01:43,147
squared distance four. It lies on a sphere of
radius two.

25
00:01:43,507 --> 00:01:46,607
The next point is three units from the center.
Squaring three gives nine.

26
00:01:46,707 --> 00:01:51,227
It is greater than four, the square of radius
two, so the point is outside. Squaring

27
00:01:51,387 --> 00:01:55,827
and adding all three component differences
performs the same test in any direction.

28
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:59,630
A sphere has three center components and a
radius.

29
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:02,990
Compute squared length of point minus center.

30
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:09,530
Take center one, two, three and radius two.
Squared radius is four.

31
00:02:09,910 --> 00:02:15,290
At the center, squared distance is zero:
inside because it is smaller than four.

32
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:20,590
At three, two, three, the x displacement is
two.

33
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:22,730
Squared distance four puts the point on the
surface.

34
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:26,610
At four, two, three, squared distance is nine:
outside.

35
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:31,430
No square root is needed for this comparison.

36
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:37,490
Equality defines the surface; less than or
equal defines the solid.

37
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:42,810
Validate finite coordinates, a nonnegative
radius and a tolerance with defined units.

38
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:49,380
Doubling radius doubles diameter, but area and
volume grow differently.

39
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:55,020
Diameter is two r and circumference is two pi
r. Both have length units.

40
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:56,960
Circle area is pi r squared; sphere surface
area is four pi r squared.

41
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:01,620
Both use squared length units.

42
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,440
Sphere volume is four thirds pi r cubed.

43
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:08,240
Cubic units make volume grow eightfold when
radius doubles.

44
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,060
Surface area grows fourfold. Calling length,

45
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:15,200
area and volume simply size can cause
incorrect comparisons.

46
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,220
When changing a game radius, distinguish what
remains constant. Effects, tests and particle

47
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:26,160
counts are design choices; these formulas
alone do not determine game performance.

48
00:03:26,833 --> 00:03:29,933
We tested sphere distance and size changes.

49
00:03:30,173 --> 00:03:35,193
A large sphere around a long bridge also
includes substantial empty space beside it.

50
00:03:35,493 --> 00:03:40,933
Record the object extent separately along each
axis to reduce that space.

51
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:44,773
Look for these three ranges in the next bodies
and bridge.

52
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:49,200
Small and large enemies move through the
village.

53
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:53,020
Imagine checking every head, arm and torso
detail.

54
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:57,656
Detailed comparisons across poses involve many
data points.

55
00:03:57,856 --> 00:04:01,372
Simple bounding shapes can first filter
candidates.

56
00:04:01,672 --> 00:04:05,452
Think of a sphere around a round head or a box
around a tall body.

57
00:04:05,812 --> 00:04:10,132
These are our illustrative proxies, not
verified game colliders.

58
00:04:10,172 --> 00:04:14,648
Distinguish a box following object orientation
from a box parallel to world axes.

59
00:04:14,648 --> 00:04:17,748
They can enclose different amounts of empty
space.

60
00:04:18,464 --> 00:04:21,504
Turning or extending arms changes what must be
enclosed.

61
00:04:21,984 --> 00:04:26,064
A bound built for one static pose may not
contain every animation pose.

62
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:29,540
Our diagram will simplify the shape to a point
set and

63
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:32,560
independently find minimum and maximum along
three axes.

64
00:04:33,747 --> 00:04:37,820
An axis-aligned bounding box, or AABB,

65
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:40,900
encloses points with six faces parallel to x,
y and z.

66
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:45,100
Store each axis minimum and maximum.

67
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:49,100
The three minima need not belong to one actual
input point.

68
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:51,420
Center is the average of minimum and maximum.

69
00:04:51,474 --> 00:04:56,400
Half-extents are half their difference;
distinguish them from full size.

70
00:04:56,580 --> 00:04:59,560
For membership, compare x with its x interval,

71
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:03,340
y with its y interval and z with its z
interval.

72
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:06,020
All three tests must pass. A copy error
comparing y against

73
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:11,920
x bounds can compile while giving incorrect
results.

74
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:14,980
An oriented bounding box, or OBB, follows
object orientation.

75
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:20,180
Extra orientation data can reduce empty space
around a slender shape.

76
00:05:20,817 --> 00:05:26,737
Before comparing three coordinates, place two,
four and seven on one axis.

77
00:05:27,197 --> 00:05:31,617
Minimum is two and maximum seven. Their
average is center four point five,

78
00:05:31,617 --> 00:05:34,757
the full extent five, and half-extent two
point five.

79
00:05:35,037 --> 00:05:39,457
Repeat this comparison independently for x, y
and z. Each minimum can come

80
00:05:39,677 --> 00:05:45,437
from a different point. Use this principle to
read the original five points.

81
00:05:46,067 --> 00:05:50,507
Use the book five points and compare one
coordinate axis at a time.

82
00:05:50,827 --> 00:05:53,307
Initialize both extrema from the first point.

83
00:05:53,547 --> 00:05:58,467
For each next point, update each component
minimum or maximum.

84
00:05:58,467 --> 00:06:02,527
Follow the highlighted code and matching table
colors.

85
00:06:02,647 --> 00:06:08,907
The final minimum is minus five, minus seven,
minus five; maximum is seven, eleven, eight.

86
00:06:09,227 --> 00:06:13,887
Center is one, two, one point five. Full size
is twelve,

87
00:06:13,887 --> 00:06:18,567
eighteen, thirteen; half-extents are six,
nine, six point five.

88
00:06:18,727 --> 00:06:24,287
Initializing at zero can wrongly include the
origin when every point lies on one side.

89
00:06:24,627 --> 00:06:27,767
Use a first valid point or an explicit
empty-box state.

90
00:06:27,927 --> 00:06:31,487
Handle empty input, infinity and NaN first.

91
00:06:31,787 --> 00:06:35,607
Match the iteration bounds to the actual array
length.

92
00:06:36,283 --> 00:06:42,223
Axis-wise minima and maxima let us test two
ranges. Begin with one axis.

93
00:06:42,363 --> 00:06:45,703
For intervals one to four and three to six,
the overlap begins at the larger minimum,

94
00:06:45,703 --> 00:06:52,583
three, and ends at the smaller maximum, four.
The remaining interval is three to four.

95
00:06:52,983 --> 00:06:57,343
For one to two and three to five, the proposed
beginning three exceeds the end two, so

96
00:06:57,583 --> 00:07:03,683
there is no overlap. All x, y and z tests must
pass for two axis-aligned boxes to overlap.

97
00:07:03,683 --> 00:07:07,043
This test includes touching boundaries.

98
00:07:07,343 --> 00:07:12,223
The next game and counterexample separately
examine whether the actual bodies touch.

99
00:07:12,850 --> 00:07:19,632
This is a new official Portal 2 preview.
Placing portals and pressing

100
00:07:19,872 --> 00:07:22,635
a button moves the cube. Red follows its
outline when visible.

101
00:07:23,377 --> 00:07:28,218
Picking up, moving and placing it changes its
visible orientation and screen extent.

102
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:32,900
Blue illustrates an enclosure of the observed
outline at that moment.

103
00:07:33,422 --> 00:07:39,425
The box also includes empty corner regions. A
beam contact or changed

104
00:07:39,745 --> 00:07:43,845
indicator does not establish this blue box as
the engine collision method.

105
00:07:43,845 --> 00:07:47,268
We have observed the origin, movement and
placed result. Next,

106
00:07:48,030 --> 00:07:52,370
distinguish enclosing-box overlap from actual
body contact.

107
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:57,610
Large and small enemies with extended arms
move across the scene.

108
00:07:57,830 --> 00:08:01,010
Imagine enclosing each body with a simple box.

109
00:08:02,306 --> 00:08:06,266
Box corners contain space outside the body.

110
00:08:06,506 --> 00:08:09,506
The boxes can overlap while the actual bodies
remain apart.

111
00:08:10,842 --> 00:08:15,702
World-axis boxes around slender or tilted
shapes can contain

112
00:08:15,982 --> 00:08:18,742
substantial empty space. Focus on outline
versus enclosure.

113
00:08:20,238 --> 00:08:25,058
The earlier2D box-overlap lecture supplies a
fast candidate test.

114
00:08:25,058 --> 00:08:27,438
Remember the limit of its result.

115
00:08:28,874 --> 00:08:31,594
After filtering, use a test accurate enough
for the game rule and cost.

116
00:08:31,934 --> 00:08:36,834
Choose proxies deliberately.

117
00:08:38,450 --> 00:08:41,250
Our next diagram gives two separated slender
rectangles whose bounding boxes overlap,

118
00:08:41,630 --> 00:08:46,270
distinguishing candidates from actual shape
contact.

119
00:08:47,350 --> 00:08:50,610
Tilt two slender rectangles in the same
direction.

120
00:08:50,850 --> 00:08:53,450
A visible gap separates their actual shapes.

121
00:08:53,770 --> 00:08:56,630
Their axis-aligned boxes combine independent x
and y ranges,

122
00:08:56,630 --> 00:08:59,850
producing overlapping enclosures.

123
00:09:00,010 --> 00:09:03,710
Box overlap means a candidate for further
testing,

124
00:09:03,990 --> 00:09:06,350
not confirmed contact of detailed shapes.

125
00:09:06,590 --> 00:09:11,130
Nonoverlapping valid bounds rule out overlap
of contained shapes.

126
00:09:11,130 --> 00:09:15,390
This requires each bound to contain the entire
current shape.

127
00:09:15,390 --> 00:09:20,390
Spheres retain their shape under rotation;
oriented boxes may fit slender shapes better.

128
00:09:20,810 --> 00:09:24,770
A tighter bound is not automatically cheaper
overall.

129
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:29,330
Compare update cost, candidate count and
subsequent test cost.

130
00:09:29,650 --> 00:09:34,010
This counterexample explains the role of
bounds rather than an entire collision engine.

131
00:09:34,767 --> 00:09:38,787
The counterexample showed overlapping boxes
with separated bodies.

132
00:09:39,127 --> 00:09:42,047
A useful bound must still enclose the entire
object.

133
00:09:42,447 --> 00:09:46,687
Does keeping old minima and maxima after
rotation preserve that condition?

134
00:09:46,967 --> 00:09:51,587
Rotate the same rectangle and see which corner
becomes leftmost.

135
00:09:52,216 --> 00:09:56,016
How do we rebuild bounds after rotating a box?

136
00:09:56,236 --> 00:10:00,176
First try transforming only its minimum and
maximum.

137
00:10:00,656 --> 00:10:05,016
Rotate a square with x and y from minus one to
one by forty-five degrees.

138
00:10:05,316 --> 00:10:08,776
The two diagonal endpoints move to a vertical
line at x zero.

139
00:10:08,916 --> 00:10:12,416
Using only those points falsely gives zero
width.

140
00:10:12,576 --> 00:10:16,076
The other corners extend to plus and minus
square root two.

141
00:10:16,396 --> 00:10:19,856
Rotation changes which corner supplies each
extremum.

142
00:10:19,856 --> 00:10:23,076
An old minimum does not necessarily remain a
minimum.

143
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:29,576
For a3D box, transform all eight corners and
recompute each coordinate minimum and maximum.

144
00:10:29,916 --> 00:10:32,416
This encloses the transformed input box.

145
00:10:32,736 --> 00:10:38,196
It can differ from bounds constructed by
transforming only the original object points.

146
00:10:38,817 --> 00:10:44,140
This brief game shot uses a different
viewpoint. The same large body turns from

147
00:10:44,683 --> 00:10:47,463
a back view to its front. Watch red contours
and the blue screen extent change.

148
00:10:48,266 --> 00:10:53,675
Back in gameplay, both pose and camera can
change.

149
00:10:54,035 --> 00:10:57,475
Screen extents alone cannot recover world
rotation values.

150
00:10:58,183 --> 00:11:02,043
In the later excerpt, a nearby blue body moves
in another direction.

151
00:11:02,123 --> 00:11:06,407
Hide obscured contours and follow only the
visible parts.

152
00:11:07,367 --> 00:11:12,587
The earlier forty-five degrees was a
fixed-axis calculation,

153
00:11:12,947 --> 00:11:15,647
not a measurement here. Next, separate this
from structures viewed by a changing camera.

154
00:11:16,550 --> 00:11:19,090
We walk through wooden posts and rails,

155
00:11:19,330 --> 00:11:23,430
comparing screen extents of the same
structures from different sides.

156
00:11:23,710 --> 00:11:29,825
An oblique view shortens a long edge and
reveals another face.

157
00:11:30,265 --> 00:11:36,340
These screen changes come from camera
projection.

158
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:41,540
World bounds use fixed coordinate axes rather
than screen pixels. Changing

159
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:46,420
only the camera does not automatically change
a stationary object world bounds.

160
00:11:49,476 --> 00:11:54,556
Rotating the object itself in the world can
change which

161
00:11:54,576 --> 00:11:57,796
corner supplies minimum x, as in our square
experiment.

162
00:12:00,771 --> 00:12:04,671
Separate an object-attached box from
world-axis bounds.

163
00:12:05,191 --> 00:12:09,431
First identify whether the camera or the
object is moving.

164
00:12:12,426 --> 00:12:16,426
Our next formula handles affine rotation,
scale and translation,

165
00:12:16,426 --> 00:12:18,866
without combining them with perspective
projection.

166
00:12:19,567 --> 00:12:21,407
Rotation changed which point was minimal.

167
00:12:21,587 --> 00:12:26,667
Before the faster formula, separate center and
extent on one axis.

168
00:12:27,087 --> 00:12:31,087
The center is five and the half size is two.
The interval runs from three to seven.

169
00:12:31,267 --> 00:12:36,027
Multiply every position by the number negative
two. Finally add the number one.

170
00:12:36,227 --> 00:12:42,247
The center becomes minus nine. The ends become
minus five and minus thirteen, reversing their

171
00:12:42,247 --> 00:12:47,927
order, while the half-extent is four. The new
interval is minus thirteen to minus five.

172
00:12:47,927 --> 00:12:51,307
A sign changes direction, but extent stays
nonnegative.

173
00:12:51,767 --> 00:12:54,587
The next matrix calculation therefore uses the
original affine

174
00:12:54,587 --> 00:12:57,547
transform for the center and entrywise
absolute values for half-extents.

175
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:01,620
With column vectors, an affine transformation
is A times a point plus translation.

176
00:13:01,620 --> 00:13:05,800
Transform the center with that expression.

177
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,960
Half-extents require a different rule. Signed
offsets around the center can combine,

178
00:13:11,153 --> 00:13:14,880
so maximum spread sums absolute contributions.

179
00:13:14,959 --> 00:13:20,000
New half-extents are componentwise absolute A
times original half-extents.

180
00:13:20,260 --> 00:13:23,180
This absolute value is not a determinant or
vector norm.

181
00:13:23,380 --> 00:13:28,220
Rotating half-extents two, one around z by
forty-five degrees

182
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:32,040
gives both new x and y half-extents three over
square root two.

183
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:36,560
New minimum is transformed center minus new
half-extents,

184
00:13:36,617 --> 00:13:40,280
and maximum is their sum. This matches
transforming all eight box corners.

185
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:45,480
The formula applies to an affine image of the
input box. Do not apply it unchanged

186
00:13:45,940 --> 00:13:49,160
to perspective division or claim the tightest
bounds of every contained object.

187
00:13:49,783 --> 00:13:52,663
In a different excerpt, pass a stationary
house. Red follows its visible wall and posts;

188
00:13:52,663 --> 00:13:59,206
compare them with the blue screen extent and
included empty space.

189
00:14:00,029 --> 00:14:05,338
The view changes to the wider village. Camera
motion changes the screen

190
00:14:05,338 --> 00:14:09,298
extent without establishing a changed world
bound for the stationary house.

191
00:14:10,407 --> 00:14:16,822
Observe another close house view. Empty space
included in a calculation box differs

192
00:14:17,757 --> 00:14:21,457
from actual wall points. That motivates
defining what the next bound must enclose.

193
00:14:22,316 --> 00:14:26,336
A long bridge and large enemies share the
scene.

194
00:14:26,336 --> 00:14:30,256
Imagine separate bounds following the bridge
and enclosing moving bodies.

195
00:14:31,512 --> 00:14:37,012
Building a box first and then transforming it
also transforms the box empty space.

196
00:14:38,328 --> 00:14:41,908
Transforming actual object points first and
rebuilding bounds can

197
00:14:41,908 --> 00:14:48,384
exclude different empty regions. The methods
have different inputs.

198
00:14:48,384 --> 00:14:52,624
Define whether you enclose a stationary
bridge,

199
00:14:52,624 --> 00:14:57,164
one body pose or all poses before choosing a
method.

200
00:14:58,420 --> 00:15:00,840
An undersized bound can miss real geometry; an
oversized bound increases

201
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:06,840
subsequent candidates. Compare accuracy and
calculation cost.

202
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:13,156
We will finish by comparing both constructions
using the same coordinates,

203
00:15:13,156 --> 00:15:17,696
connecting endpoints, point bounds and
transformed-box bounds.

204
00:15:18,316 --> 00:15:22,896
First problem: origin five, three and
displacement minus seven,

205
00:15:22,896 --> 00:15:25,116
five. What is the endpoint at t one?

206
00:15:25,336 --> 00:15:28,816
Adding gives minus two, eight.

207
00:15:28,816 --> 00:15:33,596
Slope is minus five over seven and intercept
is forty-six over seven.

208
00:15:33,896 --> 00:15:39,616
For the second problem, rotate the five points
around z by forty-five degrees and build

209
00:15:39,616 --> 00:15:43,696
their bounds directly. The x range is minus
six to three, each divided by square root two.

210
00:15:43,696 --> 00:15:47,856
The y range is minus twelve to eighteen,
divided by square root two;

211
00:15:47,856 --> 00:15:53,276
z remains minus five to eight. Transforming
the original box produces a wider enclosure.

212
00:15:53,496 --> 00:15:57,396
Empty space explains why point-set bounds can
differ from transformed-box bounds.

213
00:15:57,816 --> 00:16:01,656
Check the input before interpreting the
result.

214
00:16:01,956 --> 00:16:04,876
We connected shape representations, parameter
domains and units,

215
00:16:04,876 --> 00:16:07,996
sphere tests, and box construction and
transformation.

216
00:16:08,536 --> 00:16:12,256
Next we will calculate plane distances and
positions within triangles.
